      "type": "text",
      "title": "",
      "description": "How to read this dashboard.",
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "### Kyverno: admission policies for every Pod\n**What it does here.** The **admission controller** sees create/update requests in *every* namespace (kube-system\nincluded) and runs: the 12 Pod Security Standards *baseline* `ValidatingPolicies` in **Audit** mode (violations are\nrecorded in PolicyReports, nothing is blocked), the `MutatingPolicy pod-zone-label` (adds\n`topology.kubernetes.io/zone=k8s-local` to new Pods) and serves the webhooks. The **background controller** runs the\n`GeneratingPolicy mcp-read-crds` (one ClusterRole per CRD for the MCP servers); the **reports controller** re-scans existing\nresources hourly for the PolicyReports; the **cleanup controller** handles TTL cleanup. CronJob `pod-zone-label-sweeper`\n(every 5 min) labels Pods the webhook missed.\n\n**Why it matters.** Every policy that matches Pods uses `failurePolicy: Ignore`, so a slow or dead Kyverno does not block\nthe cluster — it silently skips labels and audit results. Kubernetes cuts a webhook at 10 s.\n\n**Healthy looks like:** all 4 controllers UP, admission p99 well under 1 s, 0 denied requests (Audit mode), mutation\nresults `pass`, sweeper ran in the last 5-10 min. Policy *failures* are expected: they are the audit findings to fix.\n\n**When something is red:** latency → the runbook in the [monitoring guide](https://kyverno.io/docs/monitoring/)\n(`kubectl --context k8s01 top pod -n kyverno`, events); controller DOWN → `kubectl --context k8s01 -n kyverno get pods`;\nfindings → `kubectl --context k8s01 get policyreports -A`.",
        "code": {
          "language": "markdown",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        }
      },
      "id": 1,
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 9
      }
    },
